  "page1": {
    "title": "Stacking Blocks in Space",
    "subtitle": "How counting cubes and changing your view build real space sense.",
    "body": "Look at a small tower of blocks. We call each block a cube and stack cubes into shapes. Counting cubes helps us understand space.\n\nImagine a stack with two cubes on the bottom and one on top. There are three cubes in all, even when one hides behind another. The hidden cube is still there, so we must count it too.\n\nNow look at the same stack from the front. You see a tall side with two cubes. From the top, you see the flat shape the cubes make. From the side, the view changes, because your eyes move.\n\nEach view shows the same solid in a different way. When we <mark>picture it in our mind</mark>, we build space sense. Space sense helps us read maps and pack boxes. So stacking blocks is more than play. It teaches us to see every side.",
    "illustration": "assets/illustrations/venus/2026-07/08.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "A stack of cubes seen from the front, the top, and the side, with one hidden cube behind another.",
    "glossary": [
      {
        "term": "solid",
        "ko": "입체 (도형)"
      }
    ]
  },
